WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.582 --> 00:00:02.950
(gentle music)

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:05.100
- I remember the first
time that I snorkel.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:11.330
We jump in the water and
we saw many colorful fish.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.203
And it was unbelievable.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.160
So since then I wanted to
repeat that experience again.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.010
It wasn't until I turned 24
when I had the opportunity

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.340
to travel all around the
Baja California Peninsula.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.730
And that was the moment that I realized

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.330
that I wanted to be an explorer.

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:47.540
My name is Octavio Aburto.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.550
I am a conservation biologist

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.907
and a National Geographic Explorer.

00:00:51.907 --> 00:00:53.770
(gentle music)

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.720
I am study mangroves.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.280
Mangroves are trees that live

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.773
where the ocean meets the coast.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:06.353
And they are special because
they grow in saltwater.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.180
Here in Mexico, I think we have
the most beautiful mangroves

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.163
in all the planet.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.550
They are not only beautiful,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.110
they provide a lot of economic benefits

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.763
for coastal communities.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.330
The mangrove roots come into the water,

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:33.040
and they form a refuge
for many, many baby fish.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.203
Then they can live inside
these roots like a nursery.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.630
It's like a playground for them.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.213
(upbeat music)

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:49.400
I just saw a big school of
snappers and a lot of parrotfish.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.710
These juveniles only live
here inside the mangroves,

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.650
and eventually when they grow up

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:58.083
they will supply the fish
markets here in La Paz city.

00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:00.957
(water gurgling)

00:02:00.957 --> 00:02:03.550
Mangroves protect coastal areas

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.110
against hurricanes and erosion.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:10.250
They also filter water
for many coastal towns,

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.325
and provide essential
habitat for many species.

00:02:14.325 --> 00:02:17.320
(gentle music)

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.700
Unfortunately in the last 50 years,

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.133
we have lost half of
the mangroves worldwide.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.910
And this is because of
agriculture, shrimp farming,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.660
coastal development, and pollution.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.190
The first step to help mangroves

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.480
is to know how much mangrove we have

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.080
in order to tackle this problem.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.823
My research involves two techniques.

00:02:46.823 --> 00:02:50.210
One is counting everything that we have

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.940
inside the mangrove roots,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.840
and the other branch of our research

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.293
is to estimate the cover of the mangroves.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:05.416
To estimate the mangrove
cover, we are using drones.

00:03:05.416 --> 00:03:08.541
(upbeat music)

00:03:08.541 --> 00:03:10.230
(drone beeping)

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.603
With drone technology, we
are surveying areas faster,

00:03:14.644 --> 00:03:16.728
and it's giving us a new perspective.

00:03:16.728 --> 00:03:19.478
(drone whirring)

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:25.453
We fly at different heights,
at 100 meters and 10 meters,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.723
and we take a lot of images.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.780
Later we use all of these images
to create a composite image

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.190
that is called orthomosaic.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:44.480
So you can use or we can use these images,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.620
not only to estimate the area,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.483
also the volume of the forest.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:58.310
Using all this data, now we know where are

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:03.110
all these mangrove areas,
how many animals live there,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.650
and we can set conservation priorities

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.493
to protect this beautiful life.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.980
The involvement of local
communities is essential

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.803
to stop mangrove deforestation.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.310
If they understand that these
roots protect their towns,

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.890
create habitat for the fish that they sell

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:33.560
in the fish markets, we will
have not only more mangrove

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.900
in the future, we will have
more economic benefits for them,

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.500
and for the rest of the country.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:46.809
Mangroves represent a very,
very tiny area in this planet,

00:04:46.809 --> 00:04:48.550
(gentle music)

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:53.163
but they provide a huge
amount of services for humans.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.493
So we need to protect them
for the future generations.

00:05:07.196 --> 00:05:09.946
(water gurgling)

